  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 AM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nMid-level water vapor imagery shows a longwave troughing pattern \nover the central CONUS with one shortwave coming off the Rockies \ninto the Central/Southern Plains and another shortwave over Alberta \nand Saskatchewan. Moisture streaming out of the southern wave has \nbrought high clouds across the area, although this doesn't appear to \nhave much impact on low temperatures this morning as we remain on \ntrack to hit the mid 20s for most places. Regional radar shows light \nreflectivity across the TX/OK panhandles up into the High Plains of \neastern CO and western KS. Surface high pressure remains in control \nacross MN and IA, and this maintains enough influence on our weather \ntoday to keep the seasonally cool and dry air mass in place, which \nin turn keeps the light snowfall confined to western KS this morning \nbefore it falls apart. Between the persistent high cloud cover and \nthe low-level wind pattern poised to keep cool air around, \ntemperatures will struggle to get above 40 degrees in far \nnortheastern KS while most other places still only reach the low \n40s. \n\nWhile the southern shortwave moves onto the Arklatex region, the \nnorthern one becomes a compact low as it dives across the Northern \nPlains into the MO River Valley tonight into Wednesday. The GFS and \nEuro are still trying to generate light QPF with this system in \neastern NE/KS, and the RRFS and individual REFS members try to bring \nlight and spotty reflectivity into the area Wednesday as well. For \nnow, the HRRR keeps precip just outside of our forecast area. The \nbiggest question with this system is the moisture quality. The HRRR \nlikely fails to produce precip this far west because its soundings \ntend to keep more dry air through the column. This is especially \ntrue as you go further west away from the center of the low. The \nRRFS/REFS soundings, however, show a bit of a different story. They \nare in decent agreement on the mid-levels being saturated with a dry \nlayer at 850mb to the sfc starting Wednesday morning. They vary on \nhow well that dry layer saturates as the day goes on, but there \ncould be a brief time period when enough frontogenetical lift can \nsqueeze out some snow flurries provided the moisture is there. The \nNBM has kept a dry forecast and am fine with that for now given the \nnumber of soundings that have dry air in the low levels. Will need \nto monitor trends and consider adjusting if necessary, but the good \nnews is this shouldn't lead to meaningful impacts or accumulation if \nit does precipitate.\n\nThat upper low eventually phases with the southern wave and becomes \na more dynamic system in the Mississippi and Ohio River Valleys on \nThanksgiving. With our area being on the subsident side, we simply \nstay cool and dry with highs a few degrees warmer but still in the \n40s. \n\nBy Friday, downslope flow should allow for temperatures to return to \nthe 50s. Guidance begins to diverge on how amplified the pattern is \nheading into the weekend, which will have some impact on \ntemperatures as well as precip chances. A deeper trough would bring \na stronger wave through the area on Saturday, though this is low \nconfidence with PoPs limited to 15-20%. Temperatures would be well \nwarm enough to support rain by this point if it does occur. A weaker \npattern, which is more common in ensemble clusters, would keep dry \nweather through the weekend into early next week.",
